      passageTitle: "The Night Watchman",
      passage: "<p>Ever since the new custodian, Mr. Voss, started working the night shift, strange things had been happening in the robotics lab. Soldering irons vanished from their pegboard hooks. A crate of servo motors turned up shoved behind the recycling bin. Twice, Priya arrived before first bell to find the lab door unlocked, though the sign-out sheet showed no one had checked it out. She noticed that Mr. Voss always seemed to linger near the lab windows after dark, sweeping the same stretch of hallway long after the rest of the building had gone quiet, his flashlight beam sliding across the glass like he was searching for something—or someone.</p><p>Priya mentioned it to her robotics partner, Diego, during lunch. \"He's always right outside our door,\" she whispered. \"And last Tuesday I saw him climb in through the supply window instead of using his key.\" Diego frowned but said nothing. That was enough for Priya. She decided that if the team's regional competition parts kept disappearing, she would catch the thief herself. She told Diego she was staying late to finish wiring a sensor array, then hid inside the supply closet, leaving the door cracked just enough to see the workbenches and the tall shadow she was certain would eventually appear.</p><p>Near nine o'clock, the hallway lights clicked off in sequence, and Mr. Voss slipped into the lab carrying a canvas bag. Priya's pulse jumped. But instead of touching the parts bins, he knelt beside the tool cabinet and pried off a rear panel, revealing a tangle of wiring that hissed and sparked faintly in the dark. He worked quickly, clipping a frayed wire and taping a scorched outlet, muttering about a fire hazard he'd flagged in his maintenance log three weeks earlier. From his bag he pulled the missing servo motors and soldering irons, setting them gently on a shelf far from the sparking panel. \"Should've moved these sooner,\" he murmured to no one, checking his watch before easing the panel back into place.</p><p>Priya stepped out of the closet, cheeks burning with embarrassment. Mr. Voss explained that he'd noticed the electrical fault during his very first week and worried the exposed wiring might spark near flammable supplies, so he'd quietly relocated anything valuable until the district approved repairs. He'd also noticed items going missing on nights he wasn't even in the building—someone else had been slipping in through the same supply window. He'd been logging times and dates, waiting for enough evidence before reporting a student rather than making an accusation he couldn't prove.</p><p>The next morning, the assistant principal reviewed Mr. Voss's log alongside the hallway camera near the supply window and identified Jasper, a ninth grader who had been borrowing parts for a secret project of his own. Jasper returned everything and apologized to the team. Priya apologized too, this time to Mr. Voss, who only shrugged and said everyone jumps to conclusions when they're missing evidence. Watching him wheel his cart down the hall, she realized the real lesson wasn't about the missing parts at all—it was about how easily suspicion could point at the wrong person entirely.</p>",
