      name: "Reading: Informational Text 1",
      passageTitle: "How Your Immune System Learns to Fight",
      passage: "<p>Every day, the human body fends off thousands of viruses and bacteria without its owner ever noticing, thanks to a defense network scientists call the immune system. This system is not one single mechanism but two overlapping ones: an older, faster set of defenses and a newer, more precise set that takes days to fully activate.</p><p>The first line of defense, known as innate immunity, is nonspecific, meaning it attacks any invader the same way regardless of what it is. Skin and mucus membranes physically block most germs from entering the body at all. If a pathogen slips past these barriers, patrolling white blood cells called neutrophils and macrophages engulf and digest it within minutes to hours. Innate immunity reacts fast, but it cannot remember an intruder from one infection to the next.</p><p>The second line of defense, adaptive immunity, works more slowly but far more precisely. Specialized white blood cells called B cells manufacture antibodies, proteins shaped to latch onto one specific antigen, a molecule unique to a particular pathogen. Other cells, called T cells, seek out and destroy the body's own cells once they have been hijacked by a virus. Building this precise response typically takes a week or more the first time the body meets a new pathogen, which is why early symptoms of an unfamiliar illness can be severe. Crucially, once the battle ends, a small population of memory B and T cells remains behind, ready to recognize that same pathogen instantly if it ever returns.</p><p>Vaccines take advantage of this memory system without requiring a person to suffer through the original illness. A vaccine introduces the immune system to a harmless piece of a pathogen, such as a weakened virus, an inactivated toxin, or genetic instructions for building one recognizable protein, prompting B and T cells to respond and form memory cells just as they would after a real infection. If the actual pathogen shows up later, the adaptive immune system already recognizes it and mounts a fast, powerful response before the person ever becomes seriously ill.</p><p>Before any vaccine reaches the public, it passes through years of clinical trials that measure both safety and effectiveness across thousands of volunteers, and health agencies continue monitoring it afterward. Vaccines also produce a community-level benefit called herd immunity: when a large share of a population is immune, a pathogen has fewer people to infect, which slows its spread and indirectly shields those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborns or people with certain medical conditions. In this way, an individual choice to be vaccinated also contributes, in a small but real way, to the health of the surrounding community.</p>",
