      passageTitle: "Building the Great Pyramid",
      passage: "<p>Rising from the desert plateau outside modern Cairo, the Great Pyramid of Giza has stood for roughly forty-five hundred years, built as a tomb for the pharaoh Khufu around 2560 BCE. Long before cranes, engines, or metal machinery existed, ancient Egyptian builders assembled over two million stone blocks into a structure whose base is aligned to true north with an error of only a few hundredths of a degree, a level of precision that still puzzles engineers today.</p><p>Most of the pyramid's core is made of local limestone, quarried directly from the plateau and moved short distances on wooden sledges dragged over wetted sand, a technique that reduced friction enough for teams of workers to haul multi-ton blocks. The massive granite blocks lining the king's chamber, however, came from quarries near Aswan, more than five hundred miles up the Nile. Workers floated these blocks downriver on barges during the annual flood season, when high water let them approach the construction site directly, then hauled the blocks the final stretch on land.</p><p>For decades, popular imagination pictured enslaved masses forced to build the pyramid under the whip. Archaeological excavations of a nearby workers' village have revealed a different picture. Skeletal remains show evidence of set bones and healed fractures, suggesting workers received medical care. Bakeries and breweries uncovered at the site produced enormous quantities of bread and beer, part of the rations paid to laborers. Many researchers now believe the workforce combined permanent skilled crews with rotating groups of farmers who worked on the pyramid during the months when the Nile's floodwaters covered their fields.</p><p>Exactly how the blocks were raised to the pyramid's upper levels remains an open question. One theory proposes a single straight ramp extending outward from one face, though such a ramp would eventually have needed to be impossibly long and steep. A second theory suggests a ramp spiraling around the outside of the pyramid as it rose. A more recent theory, based on a shaft discovered inside the structure, proposes an internal spiral ramp built into the pyramid itself. Each theory accounts for some physical evidence at the site while leaving other clues unexplained, and archaeologists continue to debate which combination of methods the builders actually used.</p><p>Whatever methods were used, the builders achieved extraordinary accuracy using only simple tools: plumb bobs to check vertical lines, sighting instruments to track the stars for orientation, and copper tools to cut stone. The base of the pyramid is level to within about two centimeters across its entire 230-meter length. That combination of massive scale and fine precision is why the Great Pyramid is still studied as one of engineering history's most impressive achievements.</p>",
